/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  lkp01_lee_kesler_tables -- THE LEE-KESLER EQUATION OF STATE AGAINST THE
  TABLES THE PAPER PRINTS.

  THE QUESTION.  Lee & Kesler (AIChE J. 21 (1975) 510-527) print their
  correlation as numbers: Tables 5-12 give Z, (H*-H)/RT_c, (S*-S)/R and
  log10(f/P) of the simple fluid (the superscript (0)) and the deviation
  function (1) = (reference fluid - simple fluid)/0.3978, on a grid of
  T_r = 0.3-4 and P_r = 0.01-10.  Does the engine's implementation of
  Eqs. 3, 9, 11 and 12 with the Table 1 constants give those numbers?

  HOW A TABLE ENTRY BECOMES A STATE.  The correlation is written in reduced
  variables, so a fluid with T_c = 100 K and P_c = 10 bar IS the table's axes
  in other units: T = 120 K, P = 10 bar is T_r = 1.20, P_r = 1.0.  The two
  pseudo-components of this case are the paper's two fluids:

      LKsimple     omega = 0       the simple fluid     -> Z^(0), q^(0)
      LKreference  omega = 0.3978  the reference fluid  -> Z^(r), q^(r)

  and the printed deviation function is (q^(r) - q^(0))/0.3978.  Seven states
  are run on each fluid, each on its single physical root (supercritical,
  dilute gas, compressed liquid); the departures come out in J/mol and J/(mol K)
  and the gate bin/curate/check_lee_kesler_ploecker.py divides by R T_c and R to
  compare with the page.  Which printed entries were read, and to how many
  digits they agree, is in that gate, not asserted here.

  WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED.  The tables are the correlation's OWN output; agreeing
  with them proves the implementation, not the physics.  The physics is tested
  against measurement in lkp02_n2_ch4_ar_112K.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name        simple_A;
        type        propertyPoint;
        state       { T 120 K;  P 10 bar;  composition { LKsimple 1.0; } }
        properties  ( Z  H_R  S_R );
    }
    {
